{
  "labId": "ucsd-pediatrics-lab-haddad-lab",
  "status": "supported",
  "summaryZh": "Haddad 实验室研究缺氧和高碳酸血症对细胞功能及发育的影响，重点是神经元和胶质细胞的死亡、存活及低氧耐受机制。方法未明确。",
  "researchQuestions": [
    "缺氧时，细胞死亡或存活涉及哪些遗传和分子机制？",
    "哪些机制影响细胞对低氧环境的耐受或易感性？"
  ],
  "topics": [
    "缺氧与细胞存活",
    "神经元与胶质细胞",
    "低氧适应"
  ],
  "methods": [],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "sourceId": "src-f8e79b6627c0668f",
      "quote": "Haddad Lab Research Focus The overall interest of Dr. Haddad's laboratory is the effect of hypoxia (and hypercapnia) on cell function and development. Mammalian tissues are extremely sensitive to the stress of hypoxia and can only survive for relatively short periods of time. In particular, the laboratory is interested in the genetic and molecular mechanisms of cell death and cell survival in oxygen deprivation as well as the mechanisms of tolerance and susceptibility to low oxygen environment, especially in nerve cells and glia. Furthermore, we have also been interested in long term hypoxia and in the adaptation of cells and tissues to this stress, in the hope that we can learn enough from the tolerant/adapted cells and tissues to be able to manipulate sensitive tissues to become more tolerant and less likely to be injured during hypoxic stress. Gabriel G. Haddad, M.D. Gabriel G. Haddad, M.D., is Distinguished Professor of Pediatrics and Neuroscience, Vice Dean for Children’s Academic Programs and Chairman of the Department of Pediatrics at the University of California, San Diego. He is also the Physician-in-Chief and Chief Scientific Officer at Rady Children’s Hospital – San Diego. Research Our Team Our Collaborators",
      "supports": [
        "research",
        "topics"
      ]
    },
